{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>בן פרת.</b> A son of grace, and it is an Aramaic locution — אַפִּרְיָן נַמְטְיֵהּ to Rabbi Shimon, at the end of Bava Metzia (119a)." "<b>בן פרת עלי עין.</b> His charm is stretched over the eye that sees him." "<b>בנת צעדה עלי שור.</b> The daughters of Egypt would walk (on the wall) to gaze at his beauty — many daughters, each one walking to a place from which she could see him." "<b>עלי שור.</b> On seeing him, as in ״אֲשׁוּרֶנּוּ וְלֹא קָרוֹב״ (Numbers 24). And another interpretation: there are many, and this leans toward the settled reading of Scripture. The tav of פֹּרָת is a lexical refinement, as in ״עַל דִּבְרַת בְּנֵי הָאָדָם״ (Ecclesiastes 3)." "<b>שור.</b> Like לָשׁוּר." "<b>Alei shur.</b> For שׁוּר — and the Targum of Onkelos: בָּנוֹת צָעֲדָה עֲלֵי שׁוּר — תְּרֵין שִׁבְטִין יִפְּקוּן מִבְּנוֹהִי, etc. And it wrote 'daughters' for the sake of the daughters of Manasseh, daughters of Tzelafchad, who took a portion on both sides of the Jordan. בְּרִי דְּיִסְגֵּי יוֹסֵף — פֹּרָת is a term of fruitfulness and increase. And there are those who settle the language thus: when Esav came to meet Jacob, in all of them the matriarchs went ahead to walk before their sons to prostrate themselves, but regarding Rachel it is written: נִגַּשׁ יוֹסֵף וְרָחֵל וַיִּשְׁתַּחֲווּ. Yosef said: this wicked man's eye is haughty — perhaps he will set his eye on my mother. He went out in front of her and stretched his stature to cover her. And that is what his father blessed him: בֵּן פֹּרָת — 'You made yourself great, Yosef, over the eye of Esav'; therefore you merited greatness." "<b>Daughters tread upon the wall.</b> To gaze at you when you went out over Egypt. And they further expounded it regarding the matter of the evil eye not ruling over his seed; and even when he blessed Menasheh and Efrayim he blessed them like fish — for the evil eye does not rule over them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {3 "Rashi cites two readings of עלי שור: one taking שור as 'seeing' (root שור), another taking it as plural 'walls' (שורות) — 'there are many [walls]'; this second reading supports reading בנות (daughters) rather than בנת.", 5 "The Aramaic phrase בְּרִי דְּיִסְגֵּי יוֹסֵף ('how great was the increase of Yosef!') is left untranslated as in the original, preserving Rashi's embedded Aramaic citation from Onkelos."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:24.783158269Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}